#N canvas 100 100 700 500 12;
#X obj 40 20 inlet;
#X obj 40 60 netreceive -u -b 9001;
#X obj 40 100 oscparse;
#X obj 40 140 select streamWatcherBang;
#X obj 40 180 outlet;
#X text 300 20 bridge_streamWatcher: REPLACES js_streamWatcher_stub. Real bang\\\, not a placeholder -- receives it over OSC/UDP from bridge/streamWatcher_bridge.js\\\, a standalone Node process that replicates the original streamWatcher.js logic (polls stream.txt every 1s\\\, unchanged behavior) using Node's fs/setTimeout instead of Max's File/Task APIs. Start the bridge alongside Pd: `node bridge/streamWatcher_bridge.js --data-dir /path/to/EBYS/data --send-port 9001` (port must match the netreceive port below). Inlet 0 is unused (matches the original stub's interface\\\, nothing was ever wired into it in the source Max patch either) -- kept only so existing upstream connections\\\, if any get added later\\\, don't break., f 560;
#X text 300 220 [netreceive -u -b 9001]: opens a UDP socket in raw/binary mode (the -b flag) on port 9001\\\, matching the bridge script's --send-port. [oscparse]: decodes the raw OSC bytes into a Pd list -- a single-segment address like "/streamWatcherBang" becomes a one-symbol list "streamWatcherBang" with no trailing args\\\, since oscparse splits multi-segment OSC addresses on "/" into separate leading atoms (verified against Pd's own x_misc.c source -- this is why the bridge intentionally uses a single-segment address\\\, not "/streamWatcher/bang"\\\, avoiding the nested-route complexity multi-segment addresses would need). [route streamWatcherBang]: when the match consumes the WHOLE incoming list (nothing follows the matched selector)\\\, Pd's route outputs a bare bang on the matching outlet -- exactly the original streamWatcher.js's outlet(0\\\, "bang") behavior., f 560;
#X obj 116 100 print SW-BYTES;
#X obj 40 220 print SW-PARSED;
#X obj 300 300 print SW-ROUTED;
#X text 40 380 DIAGNOSTIC TAPS (2026-08-08). Three prints splitting the chain into stages so the Pd console says exactly where a packet stops: SW-BYTES = raw bytes arrived from the socket at all. SW-PARSED = oscparse understood them and produced a list. SW-ROUTED = the selector matched and a bang left the outlet. Whichever is the LAST one to print is the stage that is broken. Silence on all three means nothing is reaching port 9001 and the problem is transport \, not this patch. Remove these once it works., f 62;
#X text 300 460 THE FIX (2026-08-08). [oscparse] emits a SYMBOL message for a zero-argument OSC address \, not the one-element list this patch's own comment above claims. That distinction is the whole bug: [route] given a symbol message matches its arguments against the SELECTOR -- the literal word "symbol" -- never against the symbol's value \, so [route streamWatcherBang] could not match and the bang fell out the reject outlet into nothing. Confirmed from the console \, not assumed: the SW-PARSED tap printed "symbol streamWatcherBang". . First attempt was [list] in front of route \, to coerce the symbol into a list. That did not fire either. [select] is the right object outright: given a symbol creation argument it matches symbol MESSAGES directly and emits a bare bang \, which is exactly the contract this outlet needs -- no coercion step to get wrong., f 62;
#X connect 0 0 1 0;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 1 0 7 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 2 0 8 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 0 9 0;
